  41: // Case-sensitivity is a property of the platform's default filesystem, and
  42: // this key exists to model it so a mirror never deletes a file the source
  43: // kept under a different case. Windows (NTFS) and macOS (APFS) are
  44: // case-insensitive by default → fold (posix-normalize + ASCII-lowercase);
  45: // Linux (ext4/xfs) is case-sensitive → exact. This matters most for the
  46: // unified session, the first mirror path that diffs a WIRE source set (the
  47: // source's on-disk case) against the DEST filesystem: on a case-insensitive
  48: // dest the two cases can diverge, and an exact key would delete the
  49: // just-written file (review otp-6b F1). Case-insensitive Linux mounts and
  50: // case-sensitive macOS volumes are rare misconfigurations a compile-time cfg
  51: // cannot detect; ASCII-only folding leaves Unicode case variants approximate
  52: // (a known gap, same as the pre-existing Windows behavior).
  53: #[cfg(any(windows, target_os = "macos"))]
  54: #[derive(Hash, Eq, PartialEq, Clone, Debug)]
